  ],
  "summary": "The cited passages present several hopes associated with God's anointed ruler: a lasting Davidic house, a just king, and a servant who suffers. The New Testament identifies Jesus as Messiah and reads his suffering and exaltation in relation to these Scriptures, while each earlier passage retains its own setting and emphasis.",
  "historical_context": "The texts move from the promise to David in 2 Samuel 7, through royal and prophetic passages in Psalms and Isaiah, to New Testament claims about Jesus. Luke 24 describes Jesus interpreting earlier Scripture after his resurrection, and Acts 2 presents Peter's argument that God has made Jesus both Lord and Messiah. This follows the cited texts rather than reconstructing a single, uniform expectation across all Jewish communities.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "",
  "hebraic_worldview": "",
  "second_temple_context": "",
  "canonical_context": "The references combine passages with different roles. Second Samuel 7 promises David a continuing house; Psalm 2 portrays the LORD's anointed king; Psalm 110 joins royal language with priestly imagery; and Isaiah 9 and 11 picture a ruler associated with justice and restoration. Isaiah 52–53 describes the servant's suffering and exaltation. Luke 24 and Acts 2 then present Christian readings of Scripture in relation to Jesus, while Romans 1 and Philippians 2 emphasize his Davidic descent and exaltation. The sequence shows how the New Testament connects these hopes without making every passage say the same thing.",
  "later_christian_reception": "",
